    "line": 14,
    "path": "research/readiness/claims/claude-2026-10-18-codif-b1.jsonl",
    "research_rationale": "EO 12335 (Dec. 16, 1981; 46 FR 61633) established the National Commission on Social Security Reform under FACA; its nine operative subsections all concern the Commission. Section 4(b) (printed '(h)' in the OCR) ends it 'thirty days after submitting its report'; the report deadline in sec. 2(b) moved from December 31, 1982 to January 15, 1983 (EO 12397) and January 20, 1983 (EO 12402). The order has no fixed termination date, so the Commission's end depends on the actual submission date, which no held source records. Every clause is terminated (EO 12534 sec. 3 lists the order among those whose committees had terminated, and revoked it effective September 30, 1985), but the first-end date is left open rather than inferred from the deadline. The draft's FULL_AND_PARTIAL_EVIDENCE_CONFLICT is resolved by reading EO 12534 sec. 3, which revokes each listed order as a whole; the 'partial' reading came only from the OFR catalog's acting-order phrasing 'Revokes advisory committees established by'. No human certification is asserted.",
    "unresolved_material_gaps": [
      "ending_or_current_basis: the first end of every clause (and so whole_order_end) is not established. Sec. 4(b) ends the Commission thirty days after it submitted its report (due by January 20, 1983 under sec. 2(b) as amended by EO 12402). No held source records the date the report was actually submitted, and a reporting deadline is not evidence of that date. EO 12534 sec. 3 (effective 1985-09-30) is a cleanup revocation of a body it describes as already terminated, so it cannot be used as the first end. Needed: an official record of the report's transmittal date (for example the Weekly Compilation of Presidential Documents or the report itself), queued through FETCH_QUEUE.jsonl."
    ]
  },
